WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.910
Pennywise was famously first brought to life
by Tim Curry in the original It mini-series.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:09.639
If the filmmakers behind the new It movies
were going to make their mark, they needed

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.080
a Pennywise for the ages.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.260
Here's how Bill Skarsgård rose to the challenge.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:19.440
Skarsgård began developing his take on Pennywise
well before he actually started shooting any

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:19.940
scenes.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.060
He took plenty of inspiration from Stephen
King's original novel, which helped him understand

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:29.000
the balance between Pennywise's evil, cosmic
origin and its more recognizable clown form.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.120
But he had very few visual cues to draw from
as he first began to flesh out what made the

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:33.840
creature tick.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.820
This created certain challenges in designing
the character, particularly in terms of his

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:43.140
voice, which in the film ranges from childlike
to nasally and wavering to outright monstrous.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:44.500
"Popcorn!

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:45.460
Is that your favorite?"

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:46.200
"Uh-huh."

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:47.420
"Mine too!"

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.980
For Skarsgård, that voice and its many facets
could not fully come into his head until he

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.080
saw what Pennywise actually looked like.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.240
He told Collider:

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.940
"It was really when we did the full make-up
test and see what the character would actually

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.000
look like … My first test with the prosthetics
and makeup was where I saw what the character

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.689
would actually look like with me in it.

00:01:05.689 --> 00:01:08.760
That's something I needed to explore to [discover]
what this thing would sound like."

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:13.959
With makeup artists, digital effects artists,
costumers, and director Andy Muschietti's

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.760
own designs for the character, Skarsgård
had a lot of help getting into the look of

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:18.260
Pennywise.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.800
Pennywise's demonic grin, for example, is
created by Skarsgård drooping his lower lip

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.720
down so that it almost comes to a point.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.479
That's not a prosthetic or digital effect,
but simply something he's always been able

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:28.980
to do.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:30.420
"Can we see that?

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.720
Do you mind — just for that camera right
there."

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:33.220
"Which one?"

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:33.720
"Number three?"

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:34.220
"Yeah."

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:40.240
When Muschietti was designing the character,
he had the idea that sometimes one of Pennywise's

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.880
eyes would wander, and told Skarsgård that
he would add the lazy eye in digitally in

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:44.720
post-production.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.060
Skarsgård explained that he could do it…
and revealed that he could trigger his own

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:48.760
lazy eye.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.700
"What are the chances that the actor that
you cast can do that practically?"

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.760
Because Pennywise takes the form of a clown,
his costume was obviously always going to

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:02.770
be a key part of the overall look, and for
that Muschietti turned to costume designer

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:03.580
Janie Bryant.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:07.520
Rather than take the more modern circus clown
approach of the miniseries, Bryant went for

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.780
something a little more timeless, with Victorian
and even Elizabethan influence to give it

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.900
a sense of history and even immortality.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:17.840
In shaping the costume, Bryant added peplum
around the waist, a ruff at the neck, and

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.700
other assorted blossoms of fabric to create
a certain effect.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:21.580
Bryant explained:

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:25.340
"I wanted it to have an organic, gourd or
pumpkin kind of effect.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.720
It helps exaggerate certain parts of the body.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:31.550
The costume is very nipped in the waist, and
with the peplum and bloomers it has an expansive

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:32.280
silhouette."

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.600
The exaggerated effect created by the costume
gives the already tall Skarsgård the look

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:41.360
of having impossibly long limbs, and the tightness
around his torso suggests the body of an insect

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.080
scuttling around the sewers of Derry.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:47.480
Bryant also emphasized a high waist and kept
the pants short to add to the character's

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:48.560
childlike qualities.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.420
In preparing for the role, Skarsgård watched
the 1990 miniseries adaptation of It, featuring

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:59.350
Tim Curry's generation-terrifying performance
as the original Pennywise.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:03.480
He also made the decision not to specifically
study Curry, because he knew his Pennywise

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:04.480
needed to be different.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.090
To that end, Skarsgård brought together an
intriguing and diverse array of influences.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:12.780
He studied footage of animals to help him
get into the more predatory aspects of the

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.960
character, and found himself particularly
drawn to hyenas and bears as influences.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:21.300
He also drew inspiration from another iconic
clown of cinema, Heath Ledger's Joker in The

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:24.890
Dark Knight, though Skarsgård acknowledged
there were limits to that comparison, since,

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:29.460
quote, "Heath Ledger's Joker is rooted in
the real; you can break down the psychology."

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:33.210
He acknowledged, however, that the same rules
didn't apply to Pennywise, since he's not,

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:34.300
quote, "a real person."

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.180
“Sticks the blade in my mouth.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:42.300
'Let's put a smile on that face!'"

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:46.860
Both Skarsgård and Muschietti have emphasized
the "childlike" quality of Pennywise, and

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:51.510
to help bring that into the performance - particularly
when Pennywise lunges at his victims - Skarsgård

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.280
took inspiration from his little brother.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:54.720
He told Rolling Stone:

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:58.370
"I don't know if you've seen kids run like
this, but they move their arms way too fast,

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.040
and it looks really, really funny.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.050
And I just loved the way my brother ran as
a kid.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.540
It was just out of sync.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.460
So I incorporated that into the character,
and you get this really explosive effect.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.420
It's really unsettling when it happens."

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:11.680
"I'm not afraid of you."

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:18.620
"You will be."

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.460
Though Muschietti was determined to use practical
effects as often as possible in the film - even

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:28.069
allowing Skarsgård to do quite a few of his
own stunts - Pennywise is still a shapeshifting

00:04:28.069 --> 00:04:32.259
monster from another dimension, which meant
that, sometimes, stunt doubles and digital

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:33.980
effects were going to be the way to go.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:38.660
Digital effects came in handy when it came
to designing Pennywise's expanding mouth with

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:39.810
its rows of teeth.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:44.969
According to Rodeo FX visual effects supervisor
Arnaud Brisebois, the effects team designed

00:04:44.969 --> 00:04:49.180
a digital "rig" that allowed them to animate
Pennywise's terrifying mouth with as much

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:50.480
detail as possible.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:51.900
Brisebois told SYFY:

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.280
"The teeth rig was all hand-animated.

00:04:54.289 --> 00:04:58.830
We basically modeled the teeth all individually
or as clusters that could be grown and oriented

00:04:58.830 --> 00:04:59.440
in any way."

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:03.260
The digital effects team was also called upon
for the sequence in which Pennywise crawls

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:07.290
out of the fridge in addition to, of course,
the final showdown, in which the character

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:09.050
is constantly changing shape.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:10.190
Brisebois explained:

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:13.779
"Basically what they did was bring in all
the stunt performers into a gym, and they

00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:16.620
learned the choreography and they motion-captured
this.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:20.830
We built really quick CG assets for all the
characters, including Pennywise, and we planned

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:23.100
out all his transformations and the action."

